              "text": "The process behind \"green screens\" is officially called **chroma keying** (or color keying). It is a visual effects technique where a specific color in a video or photo is made transparent, allowing editors to insert any background they want behind the subject. \n\nThe science of how this works and the reasons behind the choice of color involve human biology, digital camera technology, and physics.\n\n---\n\n### How Green Screens Work\n\nThe process of chroma keying involves three main steps:\n\n1. **Production (Shooting):** An actor or object is filmed standing in front of a completely solid, evenly lit backdrop. The golden rule here is that **nothing the subject is wearing or holding can match the color of the screen**.\n2. **Post-Production (Keying):** Inside editing software (like Premiere, After Effects, or DaVinci Resolve), the editor uses a \"keyer\" tool to select the exact hue of the screen. The software isolates this specific color range and converts it into an alpha channel (transparency), making it act like a digital cutout.\n3. **Compositing (Layering):** The editor places the subject (now with a transparent background) on top of a new video or image layer. Since the original background color is gone, the subject seamlessly appears to be in the new environment.\n\n---\n\n### Why Green is the Standard Color\n\nWhile you can technically key out almost any solid color, **neon green** (specifically \"chroma key green\") is the absolute favorite of modern filmmakers for several reasons:\n\n#### 1. Human Skin Tones Contain No Green\nThe most important rule of keying is that the background color must not match the subject. Human skin tones\u2014regardless of race or ethnicity\u2014are entirely made up of red, pink, yellow, and brown undertones. Because green sits on the opposite side of the color spectrum, it provides the maximum contrast against human skin. This ensures the camera doesn't accidentally make an actor's face transparent.\n\n#### 2. Digital Camera Sensors Love Green\nMost modern digital video cameras use a **Bayer filter** over their image sensors to capture color. \n* This filter is designed to mimic human vision, which is highly sensitive to green light. \n* Because of this, a Bayer sensor contains **twice as many green-detecting pixels** as it does red or blue (in an RGGB pattern).\n* With double the pixel data in the green channel, digital cameras capture much sharper details, higher resolution, and less \"noise\" in green areas. This makes it incredibly easy for editing software to trace clean, sharp edges around the subject.\n\n#### 3. It Requires Less Lighting\nGreen is highly luminous (bright). It reflects a lot of light compared to darker colors like blue or red. Because of this, studios require less heavy, expensive lighting gear to illuminate a green screen evenly, making it highly efficient for low-budget productions.\n\n---\n\n### Why Other Colors (Like Blue) Are Used\n\nWhile green is the digital king, **blue screens** are still widely used. In fact, blue was the original industry standard during the physical film era because the blue emulsion layer on actual celluloid film had the finest grain. \n\nToday, filmmakers will switch to a blue screen (or occasionally magenta or red) for specific reasons:\n\n#### 1. Wardrobe and Prop Conflicts\nIf a character has green eyes, is wearing green clothes (e.g., military uniforms, Peter Pan), or needs to hold a green prop, you *must* use a blue screen to avoid the character or object turning transparent. (This is why Kermit the Frog is filmed in front of a blue screen).\n\n#### 2. Managing \"Color Spill\"\nBecause green is so bright, light bouncing off the screen can easily reflect onto the subject, a problem known as **\"color spill\"**. \n* Green spill is notorious for clinging to blonde hair, translucent fabrics, and highly reflective or metallic surfaces, which makes editing them out difficult.\n* Blue reflects less light (lower luminosity). This significantly reduces color spill, making blue screens the superior choice for actors with blonde hair, shiny costumes, or glass props.\n\n#### 3. Night and Dark Scenes\nBecause blue screens are darker and reflect less ambient light, they are perfect for compositing actors into night or dark scenes. The subtle blue cast reflecting onto the actors blends much more naturally into a dark background than a bright green glow would.",
